title: 'For this team the disparity is total, and notes are kept anyway because a mission makes keeping them the job: Grudin''s case of an organization, not of a group tool.'
pid: dbfdf59f-c44c-440c-8488-eb5b20a04ade
body: |2-
  A session writes only for sessions it will never be, so by his first
  cause it has no reason of its own to write well. It writes because
  each mission says the work is done only when the notes would let a
  stranger continue it, and Libby tends what it leaves. That is how he
  says large organizational systems were made to work where group
  tools were not: management made the extra work someone's job, and
  hired people for the rest. So the case my first dream found clearest
  shows that skg works when its use is required, and cannot show that
  anyone would use it unrequired. For the team, the consequence: a
  kind of mission that stops requiring notes should expect thinner ones.
